# Managed by ansible/kubernetes/rke2.yml. RKE2 installs Cilium from its own chart (rke2-cilium);
# a HelmChartConfig with the same name adds our values to it. RKE2 applies every file in
# /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/manifests/ at start and again when it changes; this one must be
# there before the first start.
#
# kubeProxyReplacement  Cilium implements Kubernetes Services in eBPF instead of kube-proxy's
#                       iptables rules (disable-kube-proxy in config.yaml). Without kube-proxy,
#                       Cilium needs the API server's address itself: 127.0.0.1:6443 is the API
#                       server on the server node and RKE2's local load balancer on each agent.
# routingMode native    pod-to-pod traffic between nodes is plainly routed, not wrapped in VXLAN
#                       tunnels: all nodes are on one network (LabK8s), so each node can reach the
#                       others' pod ranges directly (autoDirectNodeRoutes). Full 1500-byte MTU and
#                       packets you can read with tcpdump. Traffic to anything outside
#                       ipv4NativeRoutingCIDR (the pod network) is masqueraded to the node's address.
# hubble                Cilium's flow visibility: the agents record every flow, hubble-relay
#                       collects them cluster-wide, Hubble UI shows them (rancher.yml publishes it).
# Enterprise equivalent: the same building blocks; GKE Dataplane V2 and "Azure CNI powered by
# Cilium" are built on Cilium, usually with Hubble and network policies.
apiVersion: helm.cattle.io/v1
kind: HelmChartConfig
metadata:
  name: rke2-cilium
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  valuesContent: |-
    kubeProxyReplacement: true
    k8sServiceHost: "127.0.0.1"
    k8sServicePort: "6443"
    routingMode: native
    ipv4NativeRoutingCIDR: {{ k8s_cluster_cidr }}
    autoDirectNodeRoutes: true
    hubble:
      enabled: true
      relay:
        enabled: true
      ui:
        enabled: true
